<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Good career options in India are not limited to the four or five fields every list repeats
    — engineering, medicine, government, and "tech" are only part of the real map.</strong>
    Law, commerce and CA, design and architecture, teaching, skilled trades, sales, hospitality, and
    freelancing all belong on a genuinely honest shortlist too, each with its own real entry path, real
    2026 pay range, and a catch most articles leave out. The fields that turn into a
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> and <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong> are the
    ones you choose with the entry odds and the daily task reality in view — not the ones that simply
    sound impressive when someone asks what you do.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Thirteen career clusters genuinely qualify as good options in India right now: engineering, medicine and allied health, law, commerce and CA, government service, data and specialist tech, design and architecture, teaching, skilled trades, sales, hospitality, and freelancing or family business.</li>
      <li>Entry odds vary enormously and most lists hide this: roughly 1 in 18 NEET-UG candidates gets an MBBS seat, only 16.23% cleared the CA Final in September 2025, and UPSC Civil Services 2025 selected under 0.2% of everyone who appeared.</li>
      <li>The most underrated cluster on this list is skilled trades — 80% of Indian employers reported difficulty finding skilled workers in 2025, above the global average, yet far fewer students seriously consider it.</li>
      <li><strong>Which of these is "good for you" depends on your own weighting of pay, security, growth, AI-resilience, and life fit — score your real shortlist instead of copying someone else's outcome.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you already know you want the framework for judging any career on its own merits, use
    <a href="/blog/career-options/good-careers">Good Careers: The 5-Axis Test</a> to score your specific
    shortlist. If you want the forward-looking view of exactly where AI, cybersecurity, healthcare
    demand, and the energy transition are pushing hiring right now, read
    <a href="/blog/ai-future/top-careers-for-the-future">Top careers for the future</a>. This page does
    neither of those jobs — it maps the full breadth of solid, established options across every
    major field in India today, so you have a real shortlist before you apply either lens.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the wider map of every path inside this category, start at
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a>. If you want a structured second opinion once you
    have narrowed this down to two or three real choices,
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career
    guidance</a> can help you compare them against your actual budget, timeline, and risk tolerance.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
