  <h2 id="short-answer">Good career options in India: the short answer</h2>

  <p>
    Ask ten people for good career options in India and most will name the same four things: engineering,
    medicine, a government job, and "something in tech." All four are solid options for the right
    person. None of them is the whole answer, and treating them as the whole answer is exactly why so many
    students and families feel like they are choosing between a handful of overcrowded doors.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest picture is wider. Law, commerce and chartered accountancy, design and architecture,
    teaching, skilled trades, sales, hospitality, and independent or family-business work are all real,
    fundable, demand-backed paths in India right now — several of them with less competition at the entry
    gate than the four everyone defaults to.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The field most families never seriously consider is skilled trades. 80% of Indian employers reported
      difficulty finding skilled workers in 2025 — above the 74% global average — while lakhs of graduates
      with a degree in hand stay unemployed or underemployed. That gap is not a coincidence. It is a
      perception problem, not a demand problem.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why-lists-fail">Why most "good career" lists are thin</h2>

  <p>
    Search this keyword and most results either rank three or four hyped fields, or repeat a generic
    definition of what a "good career" means without naming real entry odds, real pay, or the specific
    catch in each field. Neither approach helps you actually decide anything.
  </p>

  <p>
    A useful list has to do four things at once: cover the genuine breadth of good options (not just the
    prestigious four), give a real current entry path and pay range for each, name the actual India-specific
    demand or competition signal behind it, and say plainly what the catch is — because every field on this
    page has one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-to-read">How to read this list</h2>

  <p>
    Each of the 13 clusters below is scored on the same four things, so you can compare them honestly
    instead of reading thirteen unrelated pitches:
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Check 1</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">What the entry path actually is</div>
      <p>The real degree, exam, or apprenticeship route — not the shorthand version people repeat casually.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Check 2</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Realistic entry pay in India right now</div>
      <p>What a genuine fresher earns, not the exceptional outlier number that circulates on social media.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Check 3</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">The current India-specific demand signal</div>
      <p>A real, checkable number — a seat count, a pass rate, a vacancy count, or a hiring-difficulty figure — not a vague "good scope" claim.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Check 4</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">The honest catch</div>
      <p>What the field's own promoters usually leave out, so you go in with eyes open instead of a surprise three years later.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="compare">13 good career options in India, compared</h2>

  <p>
    A fast side-by-side view before the full capsules below. Figures are directional 2025-2026 estimates
    from official exam bodies, professional councils, and current labour-market reporting — verify current
    numbers for your specific state, board, or institution before making a financial decision.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Field</th>
          <th>Entry path</th>
          <th>Realistic entry pay</th>
          <th>Current demand signal</th>
          <th>Honest catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {compareRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Field"><strong>{row.field}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Entry path">{row.entry}</td>
            <td data-label="Realistic entry pay">{row.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="Current demand signal">{row.demand}</td>
            <td data-label="Honest catch">{row.catch}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="paths">The 13 paths, one by one</h2>

  <p>
    Each capsule below covers the real entry path, current India data, who genuinely fits it, and where to
    read the full breakdown if a field already has a dedicated deep-dive on this site.
  </p>

  <div class="scorecard-grid">
    {pathCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="scorecard-card">
        <div class="scorecard-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="scorecard-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
        <div class="fit-line" set:html={`<strong>Best for:</strong> ${item.fit}`} />
        <div class="caution-line" set:html={`<strong>Watch out:</strong> ${item.caution}`} />
        {item.links.length > 0 && (
          <div class="card-links">
            {item.links.map((link) => (
              <a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="without-jee-neet">Good career options without JEE, NEET, or a metro degree</h2>

  <p>
    A large share of students and parents quietly assume "good career" means clearing a top-tier entrance
    exam or affording a metro private college. Neither is true, and treating it as true closes off several
    genuinely strong options for no real reason.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    {noExamCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
        <div class="compare-card__label">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-makes-good">What actually makes one of these "good" for you</h2>

  <p>
    Everything above compares fields against each other. It cannot tell you which one is good specifically
    for your life, budget, and energy — that is a different, more personal question, and it deserves a
    proper framework instead of a gut feeling.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <a href="/blog/career-options/good-careers"><strong>Good Careers: The 5-Axis Test</strong></a> scores
      any career candidate on five things: income ceiling, security, growth trajectory, AI-resilience, and
      life-fit demand. None of the fields above scores high on all five at once — that combination barely
      exists. The real exercise is deciding which axis you are willing to trade, and using that framework
      to score your own shortlist from this page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul class="axis-recap">
    {goodCareerPoints.map((point) => <li>{point}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    That test scores the career. If a field clears the five axes but still does not feel right, that gap is
    usually personal fit, not a scoring error —
    <a href="/blog/career-guidance/how-to-find-the-right-career-for-me-india/">the 4 Fit Signals</a>
    (attention, feedback, friction, and market) cover that other half of the decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rising-view">The forward-looking, AI-driven view</h2>

  <p>
    Everything on this page is a "good option today" map, not a "what's rising fastest" map. If you
    specifically want to know where AI adoption, cybersecurity risk, healthcare demand, the energy
    transition, and rising business complexity are pushing hiring over the next several years,
    <a href="/blog/ai-future/top-careers-for-the-future">Top careers for the future</a> covers that lens in
    full, including India-specific AI, cloud, and clean-energy demand numbers.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    The two lenses are complementary, not competing. A field can be a solid option today (steady
    demand, decent pay, real training path) without being one of the fastest-rising fields — teaching and
    skilled trades are strong examples of exactly that.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skill-portfolio">The skill portfolio that raises the pay in any of these</h2>

  <p>
    The single biggest gap between two people in the same field, on the same starting pay, five years apart
    is rarely talent. It is whether one of them built a real skill portfolio on top of the base credential,
    and the other one waited for the credential alone to do the work.
  </p>

